      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm currently overseeing the acquisition of a new collection of rare 19th-century manuscripts for our university archive here in Belgium. I'm trying to decide whether we should implement the Dewey Decimal Classification (DDC) or move toward the Library of Congress Classification (LCC) for this specific collection. Could you provide a technical comparison of the two systems, specifically regarding their scalability for specialized research collections and their ability to handle granular metadata for historical manuscripts?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The Library of Congress Classification (LCC) is generally superior for specialized research collections like 19th-century manuscripts because its alphanumeric symbols allow for far greater granularity and flexibility than the Dewey Decimal Classification (DDC). LCC uses an open-ended hierarchical structure that easily accommodates unique historical materials within broad subject headings, whereas DDC relies on fixed decimal columns which often lack the precision needed for distinct manuscript series. While DDC remains excellent for general public libraries due to its simplicity and widespread availability, the technical requirements for granular metadata in a rare archive typically necessitate the deeper subject access provided by LCC. Consequently, implementing LCC for this Belgian collection will likely offer the necessary scalability to support future academic research without compromising on classification precision.",
