    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense regarding the granularity. One concern I have is that with such an old and diverse collection, the cataloging process itself can get a bit messy—especially when we get items that don't quite fit the standard categories or are partially damaged/incomplete. Can you elaborate on how each system handles \"outlier\" items or those pieces that just don't seem to fit into the established hierarchy? I'm worried about the collection becoming unmanageable if we don't have a good way to categorize the irregular stuff.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Both systems handle outliers through \"miscellaneous\" numbers, but LCC provides a more robust framework for complex exceptions like damaged manuscripts with conflicting subject matter. In LCC, you can create specific subclassifications or use unlimited numeric suffixes (like 5500) to isolate items that do not fit standard categories, ensuring they remain accessible without cluttering the main hierarchy. Conversely, DDC often requires extensive use of supplementary lists or compound numbers, which can become difficult to navigate if the \"miscellaneous\" designation becomes too broad. For your diverse archive, adhering to LCC will help maintain clarity by allowing precise, distinct classifications for irregular items rather than forcing them into sparse gaps.",
